WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.280
Google wants to hack our election. Google could well elect the next president.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.040
They have a monopoly on information&nbsp;
on what you know and what I know.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.680
Fight against this threat to our democracy. As the 2020 presidential election heats up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.800
alarmism over Big Tech's influence on&nbsp;
voters is once again reaching a fever pitch.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.320
Democrats fear the impact of so-called fake news&nbsp;and Russian bots while Republicans complain of

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.920
supposed anti-conservative bias and censorship&nbsp;on platforms like Google, Twitter, and Facebook.&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.880
They all agree on one thing: the federal&nbsp;
government needs more power over online speech.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.560
We will hold social media&nbsp;platforms accountable.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.160
Russia was running all those bots about me.They treat conservatives and Republicans very&nbsp;unfairly.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.235
It's not right. It's&nbsp;not fair. It may not be legal.

00:00:50.235 --> 00:00:56.472
Republicans have put a spotlight on the claims&nbsp;of one noted researcher and self-proclaimed proud&nbsp;Clinton supporter

00:00:56.472 --> 00:01:00.160
to bolster their allegations&nbsp;of liberal activism by tech companies.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.584
Psychologist Robert Epstein. Dr. Robert Epstein,&nbsp; a Harvard-trained psychologist.

00:01:05.584 --> 00:01:10.839
Dr. Epstein, I found your testimony incredibly powerful and incredibly concerning.

00:01:10.839 --> 00:01:16.960
Dr. Robert Epstein produced a series of studies that he claims show that search engines and social&nbsp;media companies

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.802
manipulate their users to help Democrats.

00:01:19.802 --> 00:01:22.369
A former editor-in-chief of Psychology&nbsp;Today, 

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:27.701
Epstein has called Google search engine the most powerful mind control device ever devised.

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:37.360
In 2016, Google search algorithm likely impacted undecided voters in a way&nbsp;that shifted at least 2.6 million votes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:44.534
to Hillary Clinton whom I supported. If any headline comes out of this hearing, that should be it.

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:49.129
While Google has called his work nothing more than a poorly constructed conspiracy&nbsp;theory,

00:01:49.129 --> 00:01:55.167
Donald Trump cited Epstein's research to explain why he lost the popular vote by nearly&nbsp;three million votes,

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.434
prompting Hillary Clinton to call the work debunked.

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:03.816
Last year, Epstein was invited to testify before Congress by Texas Senator Ted Cruz

00:02:03.816 --> 00:02:07.790
to warn of the existential threat that Big Tech poses to democracy.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:14.346
Democracy as originally conceived cannot survive&nbsp;Big Tech as currently empowered.

00:02:14.346 --> 00:02:26.428
In 2020,  you can bet that all of these companies are going&nbsp;to go all out. They will have the power to shift&nbsp;15 million votes.

00:02:26.428 --> 00:02:32.292
He's been sounding the alarm about what he&nbsp;alleges could be even more pervasive interference&nbsp;in favor of Democrats

00:02:32.292 --> 00:02:35.920
in the current election. You have hard evidence that&nbsp;Google is censoring.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.042
And you can back that up? Oh, absolutely.

00:02:38.042 --> 00:02:45.215
So I explain this, I told this to Congress, the study that I referred to was irrefutable&nbsp;and no one has been able to disprove him.

00:02:45.215 --> 00:02:50.996
But a careful review of Epstein's work reveals that though he has conducted scientifically sound&nbsp;experiments,

00:02:50.996 --> 00:02:56.720
his findings don't fully support the claims that he's been making about the influence&nbsp;of tech giants on real-world elections.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.400
Election by election, I can actually calculate&nbsp;how many votes these companies can shift.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.240
A big part of that is what you call the&nbsp;
Search Engine Manipulation Effect or SEME.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.080
So can you just briefly talk about what that&nbsp;is and how you arrived at your conclusions?

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:18.415
50% of all clicks go to the top two search items.&nbsp;
95% of all clicks go on that first page of search&nbsp;results.

00:03:18.415 --> 00:03:26.449
So I asked a simple question which is,&nbsp;"If people trust those high-ranking search results&nbsp;that much,

00:03:26.449 --> 00:03:33.910
could I use them in an experiment to&nbsp;shift people's opinions about candidates, perhaps&nbsp;maybe even shift

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:43.492
how they would vote?" I predicted&nbsp;I could probably shift people one way or the other by 2% or 3%. That was my guess.

00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:49.654
First experiment I&nbsp;ran, I got a shift of 48% which I didn't believe.

00:03:49.654 --> 00:03:56.000
I redid the experiment with a new group.&nbsp;I got a shift of 63% which is enormous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.520
That's one of the largest behavioral&nbsp;effects I think ever discovered.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.400
In Epstein's initial lab-based and online&nbsp;
experiments, participants spent up to 15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.120
minutes researching details on a foreign election&nbsp;
they knew nothing about. They used a fake search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.480
engine similar to Google that reordered results to&nbsp;
favor a particular candidate. Then Epstein asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.720
them how much their opinions of the candidate had&nbsp;
shifted. He called the effect he was measuring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.400
Search Engine Manipulation Effect or SEME. Biased search results can easily produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.560
shifts in the opinions and voting&nbsp;
preferences of undecided voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:35.120
by up to 80% in some demographic groups.I think when a lot of people hear things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.080
you can shift 80% of people to a different&nbsp;
candidate, that seems on its face kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.760
ridiculous, right? So you observed this&nbsp;
in the laboratory and then you're saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.520
that we'll see that same shift in the real world.&nbsp;What gives you confidence that that's the case?

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:58.563
I want to be very, very clear on one issue&nbsp;here. I'm my biggest skeptic. So when I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.563 --> 00:05:06.872
a huge number in an experiment showing a lot&nbsp;
of people's opinions just shifted, believe me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:13.120
I'm very, very, very skeptical. That's why I&nbsp;
repeat experiments over and over and over again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:21.048
I do them in different ways. They are unbelievable&nbsp;numbers, but they're real. That's the problem.

00:05:21.048 --> 00:05:26.475
What I'm asking about is what applicability that&nbsp;
really has to a real-world election in which you&nbsp;have

00:05:26.475 --> 00:05:30.720
millions of other variables that you're&nbsp;
not measuring that could be confounding it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.120
you have people that are bombarded by all sorts&nbsp;
of news sources that don't just rely on search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:42.240
ranking to decide their political preferences? In one of those experiments in India, those were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:49.961
actually real voters right in the middle&nbsp;
of the voting period because voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.961 --> 00:05:55.600
goes on for three weeks in India. These are&nbsp;
people being bombarded, as you said, bombarded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:06.400
all kinds of sources of influence, but we got an&nbsp;
enormous shift with these real voters. We had in&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.400
some demographic groups shifts of 60% or more.That's a very significant result as far as I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:19.120
concerned in how easy it is to change what people&nbsp;
say. How it changes whether or not they vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.160
I think that's a totally different question. I asked Aaron Brown, a statistician and former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.600
chief risk manager at the hedge fund AQR Capital&nbsp;
Management, to take a look at Epstein's research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.240
He says that in experiments of this sort, it's&nbsp;
not hard to manipulate what participants say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.800
and that Epstein is over-extrapolating from&nbsp;
his results. When Epstein ran his online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.240
experiment in India in 2014 on real voters, he&nbsp;
exposed them to biased search engine results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.480
for an average of five minutes and then asked&nbsp;them how much their opinions had shifted.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.360
Whether that translates to actual changes&nbsp;
in the vote people ultimately cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.520
I think there's zero evidence for that. Brown says that participants in this type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.000
behavioral research often answer in the way&nbsp;
they think the experimenter wants them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.400
and even if their opinions really do change, by&nbsp;
the time they show up at an actual polling site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:10.160
it's likely that all sorts of other factors&nbsp;have changed their opinions yet again.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.203
The people whose votes you're going to shift&nbsp;
with something as trivial as five minutes on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.203 --> 00:07:19.760
biased search engine are going to be the people&nbsp;
whose votes are easiest to shift. They probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.918
end up being people who aren't voting for one&nbsp;
thing. 

00:07:21.918 --> 00:07:28.720
So these people are going to shift back and forth 100 times before election day&nbsp;if they ever get to the polls at all.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.920
And so when he says that he got 60% shifts&nbsp;
among some demographics in that study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.956
do you think that's a meaningful result that&nbsp;we can sort of map on to real elections?

00:07:39.956 --> 00:07:44.000
Not at all. You can't assume that that's the&nbsp;only thing changing people's votes. I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.400
that's an absurd assumption. Their algorithm all by itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:53.680
it is already determining the outcomes of close&nbsp;elections around the world all by itself.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.480
Epstein has used the findings from his Search&nbsp;
Engine Manipulation Effect studies to make bold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.080
claims about real election results. For example,&nbsp;
he asserts that as of 2015, bias in Google's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:10.240
search alone has been changing upwards of 25% of&nbsp;the outcomes of national elections worldwide.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.280
Can you just explain how you&nbsp;
arrived at those numbers from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.480
experiments that you just talked about?
That's actually pretty simple. We simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:26.160
looked at the margins by which national elections&nbsp;
around the world were being won, and it turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:32.640
in national elections especially, the win margin&nbsp;
is often extremely, extremely small. Then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:41.711
looked at, again, the SEME research which tells us&nbsp;
what proportion of undecided voters can be shifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.711 --> 00:08:46.800
in any given election and just kind of&nbsp;
put the two together. That's where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:53.120
came up with this estimate of upwards of 25% of&nbsp;
the national elections in the world as of 2015&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:59.520
were being decided by Google search engine.The logic he just described is just wrong. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.320
lots of other things that influence people's&nbsp;
votes. How many change their vote because of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:11.045
their spouse thinks, what their friend thinks, an&nbsp;
ad they saw, a book they read, a movie they saw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.045 --> 00:09:14.880
an experience they had? What you would have&nbsp;
to show to show that they were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:22.240
influencing elections is you need a controlled&nbsp;
experiment. You'd need actually to see how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.880
actual votes shifted, not how many&nbsp;people reported a shift in opinion.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.080
As evidence that tech companies&nbsp;
can affect voting behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:36.000
Epstein points to a study run on 61 million&nbsp;
Facebook users on election day in 2010 that tested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.480
the impact of Get Out the Vote messages. I actually measured how many more people got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.200
off their sofas and went and voted. So they&nbsp;
sent it to 60 million people. That caused an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.960
additional 340,000 more people to go vote. But Brown says this fits Epstein's pattern of&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:56.080
emphasizing seemingly large numbers without giving&nbsp;their proper context. The Facebook study found

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.560
that only one out of five people who said they&nbsp;
voted after seeing the Get Out the Vote message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:06.080
actually did, and the most effective message&nbsp;tested increased real voting by just .4%.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:12.320
It makes Epstein's higher estimates of his effects&nbsp;seem implausible. So of whatever percentage,

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:17.600
15% or something who said they changed their&nbsp;
mind in this experiment, maybe only 3% of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:24.813
voted and maybe only .3% of them actually changed&nbsp;
their vote on the basis. We just have no idea.

00:10:24.813 --> 00:10:28.560
But it's going to be much smaller. It's going to&nbsp;be much smaller than the effect he observed.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:32.320
That's a very far cry from the numbers that you're&nbsp;
talking about, right? So why do you think that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.137
when we look at this 60 million person study,&nbsp;
that's happening? Is it the actual turnout to vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:40.880
that's so much different or&nbsp;what's your take on that?

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:48.960
Actually what that means is that if on&nbsp;
election day in 2016 Mark Zuckerberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:57.262
had decided to send out a Go Vote reminder just&nbsp;
to Democrats, number one, no one would have&nbsp;known,

00:10:57.262 --> 00:11:07.120
and that would have given two Democrats&nbsp;that day at least 450,000 additional votes.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.800
If you can shift 15 million votes,&nbsp;you can create a 20% win margin.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:17.920
In response to Brown's criticisms, Epstein&nbsp;
reiterated in an email to Reason that his estimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.141
that tech companies could shift 15 million votes&nbsp;this year is "conservative, based on what I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.141 --> 00:11:28.410
we can do in our experiments." Though his studies measured shifts in opinion and not voting behavior directly,

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:32.560
Epstein says that research on polling&nbsp;suggests that people's stated intentions are

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:37.040
excellent predictors of their votes. Because Facebook had a way of at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:43.334
estimating who actually voted, it's much more stronger result. It's one we can have a lot more faith in. 

00:11:43.334 --> 00:11:48.781
That's not a theoretical&nbsp;thing based on a sort of chain of logic.

00:11:48.781 --> 00:11:53.360
After his SEME experiments, Epstein had to&nbsp;
answer one more critical question before he

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.160
could arrive at the number of real votes Google&nbsp;was changing. Is the company actually biasing the

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.440
information it shows to users? The Google whistleblower has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.960
come out of the shadows. Epstein says the answer is yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:09.920
pointing to leaked documents published by the&nbsp;
conservative activist group Project Veritas and

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:15.440
interviews with ex-employees that allege Google&nbsp;
sometimes does intervene to re-rank search results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:19.120
or remove content it deems objectionable. We're pushing down the fake news,

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.600
we're demoting it, and we are increasing&nbsp;the authoritative news and promoting it.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:28.560
I felt that our entire elections system&nbsp;was going to be compromised forever.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:35.200
These companies are absolutely determined to&nbsp;control the outcome of the upcoming election.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:43.040
The term re-ranking is used in the documents.&nbsp;They use blacklist to either remove content

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:50.720
or demote it. There's a lot of material on what&nbsp;they call, within Google, algorithmic fairness.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:59.200
Algorithmic fairness. So simple example would be&nbsp;most CEOs in the US are men, in fact white men.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:03.737
Now if you type CEO into Google and you're looking&nbsp;at images, you're going to get a mix. 

00:13:03.737 --> 00:13:10.400
You're going to get some more minorities, some more women&nbsp;
thrown in whether that's representative or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:18.240
You see, they have ways of balancing from their&nbsp;
perspective, balancing content any way they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:25.360
to balance it just like I do in my experiments. Tech platforms have often been inconsistent

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:30.000
in explaining how they apply their community&nbsp;guidelines to remove or limit certain content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.240
but experts in online search argue the&nbsp;
technology is necessarily complex and

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:38.000
always evolving and several studies have&nbsp;concluded that there's no clear evidence

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:43.680
Google biases results for political purposes. Company executives also deny ever doing so.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:48.000
Dr. Epstein's study, we've investigated.&nbsp;We don't agree with the methodology.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.680
Epstein needed proof they were&nbsp;
doing just that. So in 2016,

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:57.680
he ran a study in which he secretly monitored the&nbsp;search results of 95 field agents in 24 states

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:02.720
after they type neutral terms such as Hillary's&nbsp;health plan into Google, Bing, and Yahoo.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:10.880
We had the searches so we could see whether they were biased in any way. We found a pro-Hillary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:17.840
Clinton bias, and I was a very strong Hillary&nbsp;Clinton supporter, in all 10 search positions

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:26.080
on the first page of Google search results, but&nbsp;not on Bing or Yahoo. That's extremely important.

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:32.560
The fact that we're seeing that bias on Google&nbsp;but not the other search engines, that says that,

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:39.280
again for whatever reason, their algorithms&nbsp;are actually favoring Hillary Clinton.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:43.760
After measuring the bias, Epstein combined&nbsp;these results with the shifts in opinion he

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:48.720
was able to generate in his earlier SEME research&nbsp;to calculate how many votes Google had supposedly

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.880
shifted in real national elections. If there is any kind of favoritism

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:57.840
in search results, we know from the SEME&nbsp;research without any doubt that that will,

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:03.760
in a big election, shift millions of votes. In the weeks leading up to the 2018 election,

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:11.120
bias in Google's search results&nbsp;may have shifted upwards of 78.2

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:17.520
million votes spread across many races. What we're seeing here is actual rigging.

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:24.160
I mean this is not paranoia. This&nbsp;is fact. This is rock solid fact.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.640
But Epstein wasn't able to determine the source&nbsp;of the bias in his field agent search results

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:32.960
and his monitoring work has been criticized&nbsp;by other social scientists in part for using

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:37.760
a small number of participants who are unlikely&nbsp;to be representative of the US electorate.&nbsp;

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:42.080
It's shifting the people who are&nbsp;
vulnerable, who are undecided,

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:48.480
and the shifts are simply enormous. And though his claims focus on undecided voters,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:53.360
his data on the bias they're exposed to is&nbsp;especially limited. Of his 95 field agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:58.640
in 2016, only 21 identified themselves&nbsp;
as undecided and he didn't determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:02.320
the political preferences of others. Isn't that a confounding variable if you

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:06.080
don't know what the political preferences&nbsp;
of people are? Because like you're saying,

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:10.000
one of the big differences between your study&nbsp;
and what Google actually does is that Google has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.200
basically a dossier on every person that has&nbsp;
all of these behaviors and they customize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:20.560
search results and things to the individual. So&nbsp;
isn't it possible that the people in your group,

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:25.427
they were seeing biased search based on their&nbsp;own preexisting political preferences?

00:16:25.427 --> 00:16:32.080
We would have ways of detecting&nbsp;
that. For example, if that were true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:43.200
then we'd expect to see pro-Democrat results&nbsp;
in blue states and pro-Republican results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:48.720
in red states. If we're seeing pro-Democrat&nbsp;results in both blue and red states-

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.975
But so you had 95 people, right? Yes.

00:16:50.975 --> 00:16:58.160
You couldn't have had more than like&nbsp;
two or three people in any given state?
&nbsp;

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:10.376
Again, I need to emphasize that&nbsp;these systems I've set up so far are proof of concept projects. 

00:17:10.376 --> 00:17:24.391
In 2020,&nbsp;I think there's more at stake here and I think that we want to have numbers that aren't just  

00:17:24.391 --> 00:17:31.360
statistically valid. We want to have numbers that&nbsp;
are psychologically valid and that means having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:39.040
much, much larger number of field agents. What&nbsp;
we need are data, literally millions of pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:45.840
of data coming in every day from a very diverse&nbsp;group of people whose demographics we know.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:50.560
Why are you comfortable giving these&nbsp;
number estimates now? Why not just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:53.680
"This is a significant effect. We don't&nbsp;
know what effect it will have in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:57.280
real world. We think it'll shift it this way&nbsp;
and we're going to wait to see?" Why do you

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:02.599
give these numbers where you're extrapolating&nbsp;directly from your data to the real world?

00:18:02.599 --> 00:18:09.600
I'm very careful when I make extrapolations.&nbsp;
I'm very careful to word things in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:17.440
I think has integrity. So what I said&nbsp;
in 2016 was that if the level of bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:25.164
that we found in the sample we collected, if&nbsp;
that level of bias had been present nationwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.164 --> 00:18:35.231
that would have shifted somewhere between 2.6 and&nbsp;
10.4 million votes to Hillary Clinton. I stand by&nbsp;that.

00:18:35.231 --> 00:18:40.160
I've never said I know that level of bias&nbsp;was present nationwide. I've never said that.

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:46.320
You testified before this committee&nbsp;
that Google's manipulation of votes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:51.360
gave at least 2.6 million&nbsp;
additional votes to Hillary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:56.880
Clinton in the year 2016. Is that correct?
I must correct you. The 2.6 million is a

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:05.520
rock bottom minimum. The range is between 2.6&nbsp;
and 10.4 million depending on how aggressively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:10.093
they used the techniques that I've been&nbsp;studying now for six and a half years.

00:19:10.093 --> 00:19:18.400
Again, I'm very careful in the way I word&nbsp;
things because I'm very, very skeptical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.200
about my own work. I think that's&nbsp;why my work is pretty solid.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:27.920
His results are pretty solid and they're very&nbsp;scientific. Yeah, he overstates some, but mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:32.240
what he does is present them artfully. Aaron Brown says the issue is not Epstein's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:36.240
experimental design but the fact that he&nbsp;continues to repeat numbers that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.880
only be accurate if the effects he's measured&nbsp;were the sole factors influencing voters.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:45.200
And in the context of all the things that&nbsp;
influence votes, he has not convinced me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:51.711
that this is a significant one. But Epstein writes that Brown has no idea how powerful new forms of influence can be,

00:19:51.711 --> 00:19:58.960
especially influence that people can't see. SEME is an especially dangerous form of influence&nbsp;because it is, in effect, subliminal.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:04.960
The political campaigns, I mean they're doing&nbsp;
all kinds of shenanigans on these platforms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:11.680
they're buying ads. So doesn't that&nbsp;
make a difference? And the answer is no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:15.760
that makes no difference. All of that is&nbsp;
competitive which is healthy. That's a good thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:27.200
but if the platform itself favors one candidate or&nbsp;party, you cannot counteract that by any means.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:31.520
Unlike other forms of attempted online&nbsp;
voter manipulation like political ads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:36.240
biased news stories, and even Russian troll&nbsp;
farms, Epstein argues that tech companies'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:41.200
influence is more insidious since users&nbsp;
assume computer-generated content is neutral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:45.680
and they can be manipulated repeatedly over&nbsp;long periods of time without knowing it.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:53.680
Google can take a 50-50 split among undecided&nbsp;
voters and change it into a 90-10 split with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:58.080
no one knowing that they have been manipulated. I mean my jaw is open.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:02.640
But Brown says the world is full of implicit&nbsp;
messaging and unlike in Epstein's research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:06.720
studies have shown directly that other&nbsp;
types of unconscious influence can have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:11.040
a large effect on real voting behavior. You have a top person on the ballot get somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:17.200
between 1% and 10% more votes than they would&nbsp;
get if you reversed a ballot. There's plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:23.040
subliminal and implicit messages people get&nbsp;
all the time. You don't need an international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:30.709
conspiracy or a secret group of technocrats&nbsp;in Silicon Valley to influence elections.

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:34.800
If anyone draws news out of this hearing,&nbsp;
I would encourage you to review very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:39.675
carefully Dr. Epstein's testimony. Epstein's work is once again gaining&nbsp;traction 

00:21:39.675 --> 00:21:43.600
this election cycle as politicians&nbsp;seek greater control over activity online.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:50.320
We as a country cannot tolerate&nbsp;
political censorship, blacklisting,

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:56.400
and rigged search results. My executive order calls for new regulations under

00:21:56.400 --> 00:22:02.400
Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act. President Trump recently signed an executive order

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:07.360
targeting social media and tasked&nbsp;
the FCC with investigating tech platforms

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:11.600
for violations of Section 230, otherwise&nbsp;
known as the internet's First Amendment.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:15.600
I think this hearing has underscored the&nbsp;need for Congress to revisit that.&nbsp;

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:20.640
Nobody wants to see a federal speech police&nbsp;deciding what is and isn't permissible.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:24.320
The goal here, Kim, is not to get less&nbsp;speech. The goal is to get more speech.

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:28.320
Republicans in Congress have proposed&nbsp;legislation that would largely eliminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:32.240
the speech protections in Section 230,&nbsp;
allowing the government to punish tech&nbsp;companies

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:37.440
if officials were to deem a user's post&nbsp;
unacceptable and the Department of Justice is

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:41.920
reportedly planning to file a new antitrust&nbsp;case against Google before the election.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:44.800
And a step on the line beyond&nbsp;that is a Teddy Roosevelt step.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:49.040
And it's not just Republicans agitating for control over online speech.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:53.200
They behave like monopolists.&nbsp;
They need to be broken apart.

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:58.709
Senate Democrats joined them in a bipartisan&nbsp;
effort to subpoena tech CEOs in advance of the&nbsp;election,

00:22:58.709 --> 00:23:02.720
and numerous prominent Democrats&nbsp;
including Joe Biden and Kamala Harris have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:07.360
promised further crackdowns on internet&nbsp;platforms for the content users post.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:12.240
Section 230 should be revoked immediately.&nbsp;
It should be revoked, number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:18.880
for Zuckerberg and other platforms. That's a pretty foundational law of the modern based internet.

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:24.400
Exactly right and it should be&nbsp;revoked. It should be revoked.

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:28.800
You've also said that there needs to be strict&nbsp;regulation of companies like Google.

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.600
What's your case for that? Yes, I used to call for

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:37.680
strict regulation. I've stopped doing that because&nbsp;with Google, there is actually a simple solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:43.760
to the threats that that particular company&nbsp;
poses, and that is to make their index,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:50.400
which is the database they use to generate search&nbsp;
results, to make the index into a public commons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:56.240
So Google's dominance would disappear. They could&nbsp;
still make lots of money. Big customers would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.240 --> 00:24:01.920
have to pay maybe to access their index, but the&nbsp;
point is that's a simple solution. That's very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:08.160
very light touch regulation. There are things we&nbsp;can do. The EU has been extremely aggressive.

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:12.480
If the federal government does ever step in&nbsp;to regulate, you're not going to like it.

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:17.600
Embattled Facebook CEO Mark Zuckerberg is&nbsp;
calling for stricter regulation of the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:21.520
including his own company. The irony is that some Big Tech CEOs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:26.160
actually welcome regulation because they already&nbsp;
have teams of attorneys and lobbyists at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:31.360
disposal, but new legal requirements are costly&nbsp;barriers to entry for upstart competitors.

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:36.475
I'm a big believer in the free market, but we&nbsp;
have to admit when the free market's not working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.475 --> 00:24:42.960
and it hasn't worked here. I think its inevitable&nbsp;that there will be some level of regulation.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:47.040
So would you work with us in terms of&nbsp;
what regulations you think are necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:48.880
in your industry? Absolutely.

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:52.080
Okay. Would you submit to us&nbsp;and propose regulations?
&nbsp;

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.360
Yes. We should be setting standards not unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.360 --> 00:25:01.200
the Europeans are doing relative to privacy. A study done one year after the EU passed sweeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:06.769
regulations in 2018 in the name of protecting&nbsp;
privacy found that Google and Facebook had in&nbsp;fact

00:25:06.769 --> 00:25:11.564
gained market share. It's a well thought out, crafted piece of legislation. 

00:25:11.564 --> 00:25:13.520
And you think the Europeans have it right?

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:18.320
I think that they get things right. Recently we had Mark Zuckerberg testifying

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:23.600
before Congress, begging them to regulate him,&nbsp;and the Google CEO doing the same exact thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:27.600
which is what always happens with these large companies. They can afford the compliance costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:32.160
and it limits their upstart competition. So&nbsp;
why are you confident that these regulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:36.000
would not do what they've done with&nbsp;other telecommunications companies?

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:46.960
Well I'm very much afraid that the regulatory&nbsp;
approach will not work at all partly because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:51.760
the regulations actually get written by the&nbsp;
companies typically. Breaking up Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:57.200
breaking up Facebook, they won't remove these&nbsp;
threats. In fact, they'll really just enrich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:04.160
the major stockholders, but there's a much&nbsp;
larger issue here which is how could law and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:12.230
regulation possibly keep up with technology.&nbsp;
That's impossible. Technology moves so rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:16.480
that I just don't think laws and regulations are&nbsp;
going to be able to protect people. So how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:23.120
protect people longterm moving forward? And the&nbsp;
answer is monitoring systems because monitoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:30.800
systems are tech. Tech can keep up with tech. I'm raising funds now to build a much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:36.960
comprehensive system in 2020, one that will&nbsp;allow us to catch Big Tech in the act.

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:40.720
This is going to cost more&nbsp;than $50 million. Yeah.

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:44.862
Epstein is convinced that the influence&nbsp;
he's measuring is just the tip of the iceburg

